WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.446 --> 00:00:03.235
- He's an American internet
entrepreneur and blogger.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:04.618
His first company was part of the

00:00:04.618 --> 00:00:06.519
dot-com era in New York.

00:00:06.519 --> 00:00:09.146
He has a net worth of
twenty million dollars.

00:00:09.146 --> 00:00:10.786
He's Jason Calacanis,

00:00:10.786 --> 00:00:14.369
and here are his top
ten rules for success.

00:00:16.603 --> 00:00:18.571
- Things should be different this way,

00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:19.994
and I see the world differently.

00:00:19.994 --> 00:00:21.711
I wanted to get to this.

00:00:21.711 --> 00:00:26.559
I want things to function
the way I think they should.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:28.393
It's so hard to make that actually happen

00:00:28.393 --> 00:00:31.234
that most people never do the first step

00:00:31.234 --> 00:00:33.246
which is trying, you know?

00:00:33.246 --> 00:00:35.711
And that's ninety-nine
percent and one percent.

00:00:35.711 --> 00:00:36.857
Ninety-nine percent of people,

00:00:36.857 --> 00:00:38.640
everybody knows that
things should be better.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.625
Everybody's got ideas of
how to make things better,

00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:42.934
I mean, ideas are very
easy. They're a commodity.

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:46.230
There's really not much value in ideas.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.164
Really, the person who says

00:00:47.164 --> 00:00:48.342
I'm going to take that idea and

00:00:48.342 --> 00:00:50.505
really try to make it
actually manifest itself

00:00:50.505 --> 00:00:52.678
into a product or service

00:00:52.678 --> 00:00:54.910
that people can consume and engage with

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:55.743
and fall in love with.

00:00:55.743 --> 00:00:57.541
That's entrepreneurship.

00:00:57.541 --> 00:00:58.781
That's the difference.

00:00:58.781 --> 00:01:01.496
I've always loved technology
and building things

00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:02.757
and building products.

00:01:02.757 --> 00:01:05.224
And I'm twenty years into my career.

00:01:05.224 --> 00:01:07.646
And sure I don't need to work anymore.

00:01:07.646 --> 00:01:09.678
I got lucky on a couple of projects

00:01:09.678 --> 00:01:11.814
to make a little bit of
money, and that's nice,

00:01:11.814 --> 00:01:14.077
but I love building teams,
I love building products,

00:01:14.077 --> 00:01:15.932
I love building brands themselves.

00:01:15.932 --> 00:01:19.368
So I love the act of
creating a brand like Launch,

00:01:19.368 --> 00:01:20.690
that means something to people,

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.070
or Inside.com where people saying

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.858
like you did, oh, you did Engadget.

00:01:24.858 --> 00:01:28.356
So for me I just love the
act of creating a brand

00:01:28.356 --> 00:01:31.853
and maybe hopefully doing
something innovative.

00:01:31.853 --> 00:01:34.005
And I think on my best
day I'm able to do that,

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:36.142
and on an average day maybe not so much.

00:01:36.142 --> 00:01:38.246
The expectation from where I came from,

00:01:38.246 --> 00:01:40.516
and listen, I didn't come from the slums

00:01:40.516 --> 00:01:42.246
in Bangladesh or something,

00:01:42.246 --> 00:01:43.708
so I don't want to overemphasize it.

00:01:43.708 --> 00:01:46.286
But the expectation was I would be a cop.

00:01:46.286 --> 00:01:47.119
- [Female Interviewer] Mm-hmm.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:48.529
- So I took the policeman's test,

00:01:48.529 --> 00:01:50.094
when I was seventeen or eighteen years old

00:01:50.094 --> 00:01:51.361
with my little brother Josh.

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:52.670
And he wound up going into the police,

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.021
and I would up going to Fordham.

00:01:54.021 --> 00:01:57.789
But at the time, my dad
had had his restaurant

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:00.017
repossessed by the federal agents

00:02:00.017 --> 00:02:01.278
for not paying his taxes.

00:02:01.278 --> 00:02:02.111
- [Female Interviewer] Mm-hmm.

00:02:02.111 --> 00:02:04.087
- So they came with
shotguns that summer in 1988

00:02:04.087 --> 00:02:05.878
and they closed his place down.

00:02:05.878 --> 00:02:07.504
And that was the most
traumatic moment of my life.

00:02:07.504 --> 00:02:09.663
It probably is the second most traumatic

00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:12.525
moment of my life after 9/11.

00:02:12.525 --> 00:02:14.201
When that happened that
changed everything about me

00:02:14.201 --> 00:02:15.137
as a person.

00:02:15.137 --> 00:02:17.384
To see your dad have his restaurant taken,

00:02:17.384 --> 00:02:20.308
even talking about it now
as a forty-two year old.

00:02:20.308 --> 00:02:21.141
And I just thought to myself,

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:23.141
wow, he lost everything.

00:02:24.142 --> 00:02:25.406
And he never recovered from it.

00:02:25.406 --> 00:02:27.914
That was the end of his
entrepreneurial career.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:30.378
I said to myself, I'm never
going to fail at business.

00:02:30.378 --> 00:02:32.329
No matter what it takes I'll win.

00:02:32.329 --> 00:02:34.454
Cause I'm never going to
let the feds take my place.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:36.864
He went $100,000 in debt,

00:02:36.864 --> 00:02:38.400
and tax evasion and all that stuff.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.850
It was very scary,

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.294
but I had no money to go to college.

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:42.237
So I had to work three jobs

00:02:42.237 --> 00:02:44.006
and go to school at night at Fordham.

00:02:44.006 --> 00:02:46.223
I learned a work ethic that I think

00:02:46.223 --> 00:02:48.209
made me who I am today.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:49.325
I'm a very hard worker.

00:02:49.325 --> 00:02:52.310
I'm not the smartest kid in the class.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.787
Just in terms of being
clever and having hustle,

00:02:54.787 --> 00:02:57.778
I think my career proves I'll
get it done no matter what.

00:02:57.778 --> 00:03:00.352
You can't make everything
work as an entrepreneur.

00:03:00.352 --> 00:03:01.185
It's unrealistic.

00:03:01.185 --> 00:03:03.110
And if everything you do in
your career is a home run,

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.174
that means you're not
swinging hard enough.

00:03:06.174 --> 00:03:07.757
You're not swinging for the fences.

00:03:07.757 --> 00:03:09.122
You cannot win every time.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:11.118
You should be failing two out of three

00:03:11.118 --> 00:03:13.285
things you do should fail.

00:03:14.291 --> 00:03:15.910
If you're not, you're
succeeding at everything

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.275
that means you set the benchmark too low.

00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:22.319
I knew it was time to pivot when

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:27.207
Google's results started
going from ten blue links

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:30.302
to ten blue links with
some content at the top,

00:03:30.302 --> 00:03:32.423
and a YouTube video, and some news.

00:03:32.423 --> 00:03:33.481
And all the sudden I was like,

00:03:33.481 --> 00:03:35.232
wow, that sort of was our vision,

00:03:35.232 --> 00:03:36.741
and sort of there already.

00:03:36.741 --> 00:03:39.550
Okay, no problem, we'll
just do something else.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.134
You look at what work you've done.

00:03:42.134 --> 00:03:43.448
I think the best pivots come out of

00:03:43.448 --> 00:03:46.990
what lesson did you
learn from your failure.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.015
What lessons did you learn
from your moderate success,

00:03:49.015 --> 00:03:50.300
and I have to say moderate success

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.450
is much worse than failure.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:53.701
- [Male Interviewer] It's
like the walking dead.

00:03:53.701 --> 00:03:55.130
- That's the kryptonite, yeah.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:56.280
If you have moderate success,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.415
if you can get your business

00:03:57.415 --> 00:04:00.037
to exactly the amount of
money your payroll is,

00:04:00.037 --> 00:04:02.398
your burn is, and you just go sideways,

00:04:02.398 --> 00:04:05.148
that's when you're truly screwed.

00:04:06.865 --> 00:04:08.219
You're going to take
your eyes off the prize

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:10.037
you'll be like, well,
I'm sort of making it.

00:04:10.037 --> 00:04:12.344
Then the business just goes like this.

00:04:12.344 --> 00:04:13.448
That's bad.

00:04:13.448 --> 00:04:14.534
I don't think you should expand

00:04:14.534 --> 00:04:17.360
until you have something that works well.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:18.765
So if you have something that works well

00:04:18.765 --> 00:04:20.510
it will be easy to expand.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.968
If it doesn't work, going
to be difficult to expand.

00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:25.582
So I always like to do
things when they're easy.

00:04:25.582 --> 00:04:28.062
So it will be much easier to get it done

00:04:28.062 --> 00:04:31.782
when you actually have a working product.

00:04:31.782 --> 00:04:33.495
So for example, if somebody here

00:04:33.495 --> 00:04:36.117
had a working product that did really good

00:04:36.117 --> 00:04:36.950
message boards.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:37.783
- [Female Interviewer] Yeah.

00:04:37.783 --> 00:04:40.045
- And we don't have message
boards at Mahalo yet.

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:41.179
If they came to me and said

00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:43.444
we have 100,000 users, it's working.

00:04:43.444 --> 00:04:44.277
- [Female Interviewer] Yeah.

00:04:44.277 --> 00:04:45.262
Would you like to use our technology,

00:04:45.262 --> 00:04:46.171
I'd say sure.

00:04:46.171 --> 00:04:47.147
And maybe we'd buy it from them,

00:04:47.147 --> 00:04:48.454
or do a joint venture.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:49.854
- So we would be very open to that

00:04:49.854 --> 00:04:50.887
kind of a joint venture

00:04:50.887 --> 00:04:53.180
if they've proven it works.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.122
- If they haven't proven it works

00:04:54.122 --> 00:04:55.380
that makes it difficult.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.255
Really what defined me as an entrepreneur

00:04:57.255 --> 00:04:59.861
was I just never felt I could ever lose.

00:04:59.861 --> 00:05:01.132
I had to win.

00:05:01.132 --> 00:05:03.470
I could never let the
bastards take me down

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.885
like they took my dad
down. I had to fight.

00:05:05.885 --> 00:05:09.745
And it put a fight in
me that was not human.

00:05:09.745 --> 00:05:11.510
It was like super human fight.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:16.102
Like a resiliency and a fire
that I didn't know I had.

00:05:16.102 --> 00:05:18.198
It served me very well.

00:05:18.198 --> 00:05:19.473
Some people go to work everyday

00:05:19.473 --> 00:05:22.384
to put a roof over their family's head

00:05:22.384 --> 00:05:24.062
and to put food on the table,

00:05:24.062 --> 00:05:27.423
and there's an honor and a respect

00:05:27.423 --> 00:05:29.276
I have for those people, obviously.

00:05:29.276 --> 00:05:30.930
There's nothing more noble than

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.656
being able to take care of
and provide for your family.

00:05:32.656 --> 00:05:34.883
But you can even do that with

00:05:34.883 --> 00:05:36.416
an entrepreneurial spirit.

00:05:36.416 --> 00:05:38.263
Mark Pincus talks about everybody,

00:05:38.263 --> 00:05:39.544
Mark Pincus from Zynga talked

00:05:39.544 --> 00:05:41.333
about everybody being the CEO of something

00:05:41.333 --> 00:05:42.416
in his company.

00:05:42.416 --> 00:05:44.045
You can be in a big company and still

00:05:44.045 --> 00:05:45.517
have that entrepreneurial spirit

00:05:45.517 --> 00:05:47.513
where you're thinking how to innovate,

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:48.526
and how to change the world,

00:05:48.526 --> 00:05:49.477
and put a dent in it,

00:05:49.477 --> 00:05:53.367
and try to move the human race forward.

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:55.509
I think that's really
what entrepreneurship is

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:58.159
is that you're relentless in your pursuit

00:05:58.159 --> 00:06:00.558
of making something that
didn't exist before.

00:06:00.558 --> 00:06:02.057
- Right.

00:06:02.057 --> 00:06:03.357
- Try to make the world a little better.

00:06:03.357 --> 00:06:05.325
Try to make the experience
a little better,

00:06:05.325 --> 00:06:06.815
looking at things and saying,

00:06:06.815 --> 00:06:08.974
if that was ten percent better,

00:06:08.974 --> 00:06:09.807
or twenty percent better,

00:06:09.807 --> 00:06:10.640
or two hundred percent better,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:11.991
what would it look like?

00:06:11.991 --> 00:06:14.891
And then trying to take that vision

00:06:14.891 --> 00:06:16.614
and make it reality.

00:06:16.614 --> 00:06:19.197
I have a very open policy to information,

00:06:19.197 --> 00:06:21.829
so I explain every single thing I know,

00:06:21.829 --> 00:06:24.248
because what I've learned
about leadership is

00:06:24.248 --> 00:06:26.308
the more information you share,

00:06:26.308 --> 00:06:29.543
the more you educate people
of every single thing you know

00:06:29.543 --> 00:06:31.109
the more they come back
to you with other ideas

00:06:31.109 --> 00:06:32.574
to increase your knowledge base

00:06:32.574 --> 00:06:35.763
and the less stress there is on you.

00:06:35.763 --> 00:06:38.406
For example, we use Google analytics

00:06:38.406 --> 00:06:40.456
to track how the site's going.

00:06:40.456 --> 00:06:42.643
I made every single person in the company

00:06:42.643 --> 00:06:45.245
go through mandatory
Google analytics training.

00:06:45.245 --> 00:06:46.681
Most people when their running a website

00:06:46.681 --> 00:06:48.179
they don't give the Google
analytics to anybody,

00:06:48.179 --> 00:06:49.055
cause they want to have that

00:06:49.055 --> 00:06:50.821
proprietary information of
knowing what's going on.

00:06:50.821 --> 00:06:51.846
- [Female Interviewer]
Keepers of the flame.

00:06:51.846 --> 00:06:53.845
- Exactly. And people
want to keep the data,

00:06:53.845 --> 00:06:56.344
or the sales person and they
run the sales organization

00:06:56.344 --> 00:06:59.295
wants to keep the list of all the contacts

00:06:59.295 --> 00:07:01.090
and only certain sales people can see

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:01.935
certain verticals.

00:07:01.935 --> 00:07:03.429
You know people develop software

00:07:03.429 --> 00:07:05.038
to keep people in an organization

00:07:05.038 --> 00:07:06.369
from getting to information.

00:07:06.369 --> 00:07:09.737
I always found that very bizarre.

00:07:09.737 --> 00:07:11.178
If you trust them enough to hire them

00:07:11.178 --> 00:07:12.219
and work with them everyday,

00:07:12.219 --> 00:07:14.281
then just give them every
piece of information you have,

00:07:14.281 --> 00:07:16.217
let everybody share it and
discuss it and debate it

00:07:16.217 --> 00:07:17.319
in a very flat structure.

00:07:17.319 --> 00:07:18.655
Don't create a lot of management.

00:07:18.655 --> 00:07:20.167
And create discourse.

00:07:20.167 --> 00:07:21.694
I make myself not only available

00:07:21.694 --> 00:07:23.837
to my entire staff all the time.

00:07:23.837 --> 00:07:26.750
I make myself available to my enemies,

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.059
my detractors, my fans. Everybody.

00:07:29.059 --> 00:07:30.437
- [Female Interviewer]
You're very transparent.

00:07:30.437 --> 00:07:31.707
- It's the best way to run a business,

00:07:31.707 --> 00:07:33.188
because all you do is get ideas

00:07:33.188 --> 00:07:36.352
and the work that is usually the

00:07:36.352 --> 00:07:37.994
responsibility of the CEO gets sent out

00:07:37.994 --> 00:07:40.327
to these hundreds of brains.

00:07:43.587 --> 00:07:46.770
It's idiotic to think that you

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.615
are smarter than everybody in the world.

00:07:48.615 --> 00:07:50.553
Sometimes when you're
in that CEO position,

00:07:50.553 --> 00:07:51.755
or the founder position

00:07:51.755 --> 00:07:53.287
you start to think, well,
I had the initial vision.

00:07:53.287 --> 00:07:56.411
I'm so smart, but collective intelligence

00:07:56.411 --> 00:08:00.244
is always going to trump
individual intelligence.

00:08:01.156 --> 00:08:02.973
I look back at the people in my life,

00:08:02.973 --> 00:08:04.282
and I'm just so blessed to have

00:08:04.282 --> 00:08:06.525
so many people who've been so loyal to me

00:08:06.525 --> 00:08:07.735
and taught me so much,

00:08:07.735 --> 00:08:09.509
and that also I've gotten
to be a loyal friend

00:08:09.509 --> 00:08:10.664
to them as well.

00:08:10.664 --> 00:08:14.097
And that's really like I think
when you look at your career,

00:08:14.097 --> 00:08:18.264
always aspire, and I'm not
saying I have succeeded,

00:08:19.609 --> 00:08:21.482
but always aspire to be the best,

00:08:21.482 --> 00:08:23.972
most loyal person you
can be to your friends

00:08:23.972 --> 00:08:26.918
and develop those long term,
meaningful relationships.

00:08:26.918 --> 00:08:29.956
It is the core of what's valuable in life.

00:08:29.956 --> 00:08:32.069
- So you've done Silicon Alley Reporter.

00:08:32.069 --> 00:08:32.902
- [Jason] Yup.

00:08:32.902 --> 00:08:33.735
- And Weblogs Inc.

00:08:33.735 --> 00:08:34.568
- [Jason] Yup.

00:08:34.568 --> 00:08:35.401
- And Mahalo.

00:08:35.401 --> 00:08:36.234
- [Jason] Yup.

00:08:36.234 --> 00:08:37.067
- And Open Angel Forum.

00:08:37.067 --> 00:08:38.651
- [Jason] Yeah. I love creating brands.

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:39.906
- Crash 50. Launch.

00:08:39.906 --> 00:08:40.739
- [Jason] Launch, yeah.

00:08:40.739 --> 00:08:42.323
- You're a writer in your own right.

00:08:42.323 --> 00:08:43.573
- [Jason] Sure.

00:08:44.481 --> 00:08:47.405
- Is there any separation,
or is that all just Jason?

00:08:47.405 --> 00:08:48.541
It's all one thing?

00:08:48.541 --> 00:08:49.914
- I think that's all I Jason.

00:08:49.914 --> 00:08:51.972
I used to fret over it a little bit,

00:08:51.972 --> 00:08:54.952
and then I realized I have a
little entrepreneurial ADD.

00:08:54.952 --> 00:08:57.615
I get excited, I have enthusiasm.

00:08:57.615 --> 00:08:59.357
But I'm not going to try to bridle it and

00:08:59.357 --> 00:09:01.342
put it all into one project.

00:09:01.342 --> 00:09:02.415
If I want to do an event,

00:09:02.415 --> 00:09:03.463
and I want to write my email newsletter,

00:09:03.463 --> 00:09:04.785
and I want to host This Week in Startups,

00:09:04.785 --> 00:09:07.021
and I want to be the CEO of Mahalo

00:09:07.021 --> 00:09:08.804
and pursue education and do apps

00:09:08.804 --> 00:09:10.668
and all this stuff, I'm
going to do everything.

00:09:10.668 --> 00:09:15.014
I look at one of the great
entrepreneurs of our time,

00:09:15.014 --> 00:09:16.847
not Steve Jobs, Jay-Z.

00:09:18.246 --> 00:09:20.768
He is an amazing entrepreneur.

00:09:20.768 --> 00:09:23.268
He is an artist who's on stage

00:09:24.222 --> 00:09:27.591
and he is probably the
greatest rapper alive today.

00:09:27.591 --> 00:09:28.728
In my opinion he is,

00:09:28.728 --> 00:09:30.491
and Biggie Smalls is probably

00:09:30.491 --> 00:09:32.749
the best that ever lived.

00:09:32.749 --> 00:09:33.916
Rest in piece.

00:09:35.051 --> 00:09:37.801
But Jay-Z also ran a record label

00:09:38.776 --> 00:09:41.609
and manages other people's careers

00:09:43.263 --> 00:09:45.479
and a clothing line and this other stuff,

00:09:45.479 --> 00:09:47.747
and I just think those rap artists have,

00:09:47.747 --> 00:09:49.094
and people in the hip hop space

00:09:49.094 --> 00:09:50.520
have a very good view of the world

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.187
which is why not try everything?

00:09:54.048 --> 00:09:56.741
Why not do a little bit of everything?

00:09:56.741 --> 00:09:58.791
If some people think you're doing too much

00:09:58.791 --> 00:10:02.263
that's on them. That's not on me.

00:10:02.263 --> 00:10:03.670
When people say, what do your investors

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:05.802
in this company think
about you working on this?

00:10:05.802 --> 00:10:07.758
They love the fact that I'm diverse,

00:10:07.758 --> 00:10:10.095
they love the fact that I
get to spend a couple weeks

00:10:10.095 --> 00:10:11.647
in the Launch conference, learn a lot,

00:10:11.647 --> 00:10:14.299
and then parley that
into my own day to day

00:10:14.299 --> 00:10:17.006
experience as an entrepreneur.

00:10:17.006 --> 00:10:18.336
I just love being involved in

00:10:18.336 --> 00:10:19.191
a lot of different things,

00:10:19.191 --> 00:10:21.911
so I don't fret over it too much anymore.

00:10:21.911 --> 00:10:22.777
Thank you so much for watching.

00:10:22.777 --> 00:10:25.324
I made this video
because Matthew Hendricks

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:26.355
asked me to.

00:10:26.355 --> 00:10:27.823
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:10:27.823 --> 00:10:30.295
that you want me to profile next,

00:10:30.295 --> 00:10:31.605
leave it in the comments below

00:10:31.605 --> 00:10:32.967
and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:32.967 --> 00:10:35.543
I'd also love to know
which of Jason Calacanis'

00:10:35.543 --> 00:10:38.312
top ten rules meant the most to you.

00:10:38.312 --> 00:10:39.488
Leave it in the comments.

00:10:39.488 --> 00:10:40.743
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:10:40.743 --> 00:10:42.061
Thank you so much for watching.

00:10:42.061 --> 00:10:45.644
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

